  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a layered quantum circuit and highlights the two main resources that determine its size: circuit width, shown vertically as the number of qubits, and circuit depth, shown horizontally as the number of layers. Four qubits start in the \\(|0\\rangle\\) state and evolve through a sequence of gates arranged in discrete time steps.\n\nThe circuit contains single-qubit operations such as Hadamard gates (H) and phase-like gates labeled P, mixed with two-qubit controlled interactions drawn with connected dot-and-target symbols. This shows how a quantum algorithm combines local rotations with entangling gates across different qubits as depth increases. Near the middle, a burst icon placed over one of the wires suggests a measurement, reset, loss, or some disruptive event affecting a qubit state, visually marked by the \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(|1\\rangle\\) symbols. At the far right, all qubits are measured, producing classical outputs indicated by meter symbols and binary outcomes.\n\nOverall, the image conveys the structure of a generic quantum computation: qubits evolve across successive layers of operations, with algorithmic complexity growing either by adding more qubits (greater width) or more gate layers (greater depth).",
